CAROLINE MATTHEWS:
Thank you, Keiji.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:36.330
So Microsoft gives us
the platform, the scale,

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:38.910
and the enterprise reach that
developers already trust.

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:40.440
But the next question
is what you're going

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:41.970
to build on top of it.

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.040
And today, I'm going
to walk through what

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:46.950
that looks like with Claude.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:49.830
So four reasons
why this matters.

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.140
First, frontier intelligence

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:54.720
that performs on
truly hard work.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:57.960
So reasoning, coding, analysis.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:01.170
It's not autocomplete or
surface-level summarization.

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:02.610
This is the model being able

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:04.410
to carry the work
you'd normally hand

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:07.370
to your most experienced
engineers or analysts.

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:10.400
So when the model is
strong on the hard 20%,

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:13.340
everything downstream
gets simpler.

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:16.620
The next thing is
safety and governance.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:18.060
This is built in.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:19.680
It's not bolted on.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:22.320
And what we mean by
that is safety is part

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:23.940
of how Claude is actually built.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:26.010
The models are trained with it.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:28.320
You've heard of our
constitutional AI.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.170
That is critical to the way
that the models are trained

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:32.730
and how they will behave.

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:36.120
It's not just a layer
that's added at the end.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:37.980
So the model is
trained to be helpful

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:39.750
and to decline appropriately.

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:42.450
And on Azure, it also
sits inside governance

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:43.710
that you already trust.

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:45.870
So there's data handling,
access controls,

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:48.360
content filtering,
audit, compliance.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:51.360
So you're not trading
capability for control.

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:54.510
You get the frontier model
and the enterprise guardrails

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:56.970
in the same deployment,
governed the same way

00:09:56.970 --> 00:10:00.150
as everything else that you run.

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:03.330
Three, native integration
with the Microsoft stack

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:04.920
that you're already using.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.410
This runs where your
teams already work.

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:10.080
So Claude is available
in Azure AI Foundry.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:11.220
It works with the identity

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:14.190
and data you're already
managing in M365.

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:16.830
And it's available
through GitHub Copilot.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:19.050
Your developers don't
change their environment.

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:21.120
And your security and
platform teams don't take

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.400
on a new vendor surface
they have to review.

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:26.550
Claude meets you where you are.

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:31.080
And last, it's a short path
from pilot to production.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.570
The path is short
because evaluation

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:37.290
and observability live in the
same place as everything else.

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:39.360
So you can test a prompt,
you can measure it

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.820
against your own evals, you
can watch it in production

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.100
and catch regressions,
all without having

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:46.590
to stitch five separate
tools together.

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:49.200
And that's the real difference
between a demo that lands

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:50.310
in a meeting and something

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:53.310
that you can actually
ship and stand behind.

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:55.950
And that's the through line
for these next few slides.

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:58.500
You focus on your product,
and the platform is going

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:00.420
to handle the scaffolding.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:02.820
The model, the governance,
the integration, evals,

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:04.800
observability, all
of it is there.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:06.270
So your team will spend time

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:09.670
on what's differentiated,
not on the plumbing.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:13.380
So here's the lineup.

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.720
This is what's
available in Foundry.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:18.660
Some of our best models for
coding and agentic workflows,

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:23.130
including Claude Opus, Claude
Summit, and Claude Haiku.

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:26.340
I'm going to get a
little more detail now

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:28.920
about this entire Claude
model family that's available

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:30.270
in Foundry.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.640
So the nice thing about this,
when I talk about a family

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:35.490
of models, the nice thing is
you get all of those things

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:37.950
that I just spoke about
natively within all of them,

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:41.550
including safety, the same
sorts of prompting strategies,

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:44.160
and overall, the same
environment and API calls

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:47.010
that you would use for any
of these three models,

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:49.770
which allows you to be able to
work amongst them depending

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:52.080
on what the use cases are.

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:55.590
So Claude Opus, this is our
most intelligent model.

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:57.990
This is where you go for
genuinely hard problems.

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:01.200
So this is complex reasoning,
really deep coding

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.720
across a very large or
unfamiliar code base

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:07.500
or very long-horizon
agentic tasks.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:10.410
And what sets it apart isn't
just its raw intelligence

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:11.640
for a single prompt.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:15.630
It is that ability to stay
coherent over an extended run.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:18.600
So it can hold a goal
across many steps.

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:21.510
It can recover when something
goes wrong partway through.

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:24.900
And it keeps its reasoning
consistent instead of drifting.

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:26.880
And that's what makes it
a great choice for work

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:30.000
that you can't cleanly
break into small pieces.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.790
In fact, that's what Opus
can do is help you break

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:34.560
into those smaller pieces

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:37.290
that you could then
deliver to other models.

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:39.270
For example, Sonnet.

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:41.490
So Sonnet is a really
strong default.

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:42.420
And note what I said there.

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:46.380
That is actually a great spot
to start that we do recommend.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:47.460
It's a great starting point

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:49.620
for most enterprise
work and most coding.

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:50.550
It's fast.

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:51.900
It's highly capable.

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:55.050
And the gap to Opus is small
enough that the large majority

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:57.420
of tasks never really
need to leave it.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.130
So most teams are
really surprised

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:01.560
by how far Sonnet
alone takes them.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:04.170
So treat them as your baseline
and reach for something else

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:07.200
when the task truly needs it.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:09.360
Then we have Claude Haiku.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:12.240
This is your
high-throughput option.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:15.090
Haiku is built for low
latency and high volume.

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:18.030
It's for the work where
speed and cost matter most.

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:22.290
So things like classification,
extraction, routing, tagging.

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:24.330
Anywhere there's a
well-defined task

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:26.820
that sits underneath
a larger system.

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:29.580
So when you pin down exactly
what a task needs to do

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:32.700
and you're running it at a very
high volume, Haiku gives you

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:35.310
that throughput without
giving up the quality

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:37.590
that the job requires.

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:40.980
So the rule of thumb,
prototype on Sonnet.

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:43.740
It's capable enough to tell you
quickly whether the idea works

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:44.430
at all.

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:48.180
Then move up to Opus when
Sonnet starts to strain,

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:49.740
when the reasoning
gets really hard

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:51.450
or the agent runs
get really long

00:13:51.450 --> 00:13:53.700
and it can't hold
together as well.

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:56.850
And you would move down to Haiku
once the task is well-defined

