00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:13.470
So let's chat about
the Claude agent SDK.

00:25:13.470 --> 00:25:15.180
First, what is it?

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:17.910
This is the harness
behind Claude code.

00:25:17.910 --> 00:25:20.490
So it's the same agent
loop, it's the same tools,

00:25:20.490 --> 00:25:22.980
context management that
powers the product millions

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:26.040
of developers are using every
day, and it's programmable

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:28.410
in both Python and TypeScript.

00:25:28.410 --> 00:25:29.970
So it is the production harness.

00:25:29.970 --> 00:25:32.340
We've talked about harnesses
already in this webinar,

00:25:32.340 --> 00:25:36.000
and you are building on the
exact thing we use ourselves.

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:38.430
So first, the loop
is fairly simple,

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:40.260
but it is the whole model.

00:25:40.260 --> 00:25:44.130
You will gather context, take
action, verify the work,

00:25:44.130 --> 00:25:46.800
and repeat until
the task is done.

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:47.820
That's it.

00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:52.080
So every capable agent comes
back to those four steps.

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:54.840
What makes an agent good
is not a clever prompt.

00:25:54.840 --> 00:25:58.560
It's doing each of those steps
well, and the SDK is built

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:00.750
to do them well by default.

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:03.060
So out of the box,
here's what you get.

00:26:03.060 --> 00:26:07.290
You have code execution, a
file system, web search,

00:26:07.290 --> 00:26:11.430
a to-do list, and MCP, plus
your own custom tools.

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:14.310
So the agent can run code,
it can read and write files,

00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:17.190
look things up, keep track
of a multi-step plan,

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:19.800
and reach into your
systems through MCP.

00:26:19.800 --> 00:26:22.710
So if you have an internal API
or a proprietary data source,

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:24.600
you can expose that as a tool,

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:27.510
and the agent uses
it like any other.

00:26:27.510 --> 00:26:30.270
And then there's the layer that
a lot of people underestimate,

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:32.610
the infrastructure
around the loop.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:35.070
So context management,
so the agent doesn't run

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:37.080
out of room on long tasks.

00:26:37.080 --> 00:26:40.440
Sessions in memory, so it
can pick work back up.

00:26:40.440 --> 00:26:43.770
Permissions, so it only
does what you've allowed.

00:26:43.770 --> 00:26:46.860
Lifecycle hooks, so you
can insert your own logic

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:50.010
at each step that
will always run.

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:53.820
And sub-agents that let you
fan work out and parallelize.

00:26:53.820 --> 00:26:55.890
Plus, you have error
handling and observability,

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:58.440
so you can see what
it did and why.

00:26:58.440 --> 00:27:01.440
And this is the part that teams
always think they'll knock

00:27:01.440 --> 00:27:04.710
out in a week, but it
really takes a long time.

00:27:04.710 --> 00:27:07.530
So the takeaway is the
last line on the slide.

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:09.600
You don't need to worry
about the plumbing.

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:11.610
You ship the agent.

00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:12.840
Most teams build a version

00:27:12.840 --> 00:27:15.540
of this harness themselves
before they find the SDK.

00:27:15.540 --> 00:27:16.260
They write the loop.

00:27:16.260 --> 00:27:18.630
They do the tool, the wiring,
the context handling.

00:27:18.630 --> 00:27:21.210
They might hit a permissions
and observability issue,

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:23.160
and they realize that they're
really maintaining a whole

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:25.740
separate platform instead
of building their product.

00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:30.180
That is the work you can now
skip with the Claude Agent SDK.

