00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:11.400
CAROLINE MATTHEWS: And on
that note, evaluations.

00:32:11.400 --> 00:32:13.770
This is one of the
most critical things.

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:16.620
We talk about this all the
time with all of our customers

00:32:16.620 --> 00:32:20.070
and partners about how
critical it is to set

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:23.040
up evaluations very
early and very well

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:25.800
when you're building these
sorts of applications.

00:32:25.800 --> 00:32:28.560
And what we mean by that is
the evaluations are the way

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:32.040
that you can add a little bit
more determinism to what is,

00:32:32.040 --> 00:32:35.940
as we all know, a very
probabilistic use case.

00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:38.100
And so the types of
things that we talk

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:39.660
about with evaluations range

00:32:39.660 --> 00:32:41.250
across these three
different pillars,

00:32:41.250 --> 00:32:42.390
four different
pillars, actually.

00:32:42.390 --> 00:32:46.530
But we have quality, which
is really about the types

00:32:46.530 --> 00:32:48.180
of output you're getting.

00:32:48.180 --> 00:32:50.130
And there's some great
evaluation scores

00:32:50.130 --> 00:32:52.170
that are available
kind of intrinsically

00:32:52.170 --> 00:32:55.380
or are intrinsically on
Azure, such as groundedness,

00:32:55.380 --> 00:32:59.670
which is how close is it to
the expectation, coherence

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:03.450
and fluency about how the models
are responding and the types

00:33:03.450 --> 00:33:07.050
of answers that you're getting,
relevance to the answers,

00:33:07.050 --> 00:33:09.720
being able to see how
similar something is

00:33:09.720 --> 00:33:12.030
to a very expected output.

00:33:12.030 --> 00:33:16.020
So quality is around the
outputs coming from the agents,

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:18.600
but we have to be very careful
about risk and safety.

00:33:18.600 --> 00:33:20.850
We have this kind of broken
into two areas, but risk

00:33:20.850 --> 00:33:23.250
and safety is all of
the things that we hear

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:24.990
about in the news, frankly.

00:33:24.990 --> 00:33:30.240
Anything around unsafe or
less protected types of ways

00:33:30.240 --> 00:33:33.480
that you would interact with
the models that you're using.

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:35.700
And we also have to be
careful just about things

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:37.410
that might be ungrounded.

00:33:37.410 --> 00:33:39.420
This is where we can get
some really bad information

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:42.240
that can get out there because
it wasn't properly grounded.

00:33:42.240 --> 00:33:45.990
So we kind of classify that in
terms of risk and safety as well

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:49.860
as quality along those same
lines with code vulnerability.

00:33:49.860 --> 00:33:52.710
And overall with agents, we kind
of introduce a whole new set

00:33:52.710 --> 00:33:54.510
of types of evaluations
where we want

00:33:54.510 --> 00:33:56.970
to understand did the
overall agentic process,

00:33:56.970 --> 00:33:59.490
especially as we get
into multi-agent,

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:01.740
did it understand
the intent properly?

00:34:01.740 --> 00:34:04.320
How accurately did it
make those tool calls

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:07.650
and how well were the
responses handled by those?

00:34:07.650 --> 00:34:10.560
Task adherence, is it able
to complete the full task?

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:12.780
Again, as we talk about
very long-running agents,

00:34:12.780 --> 00:34:16.410
we need to evaluate how
well it can work over time.

00:34:16.410 --> 00:34:19.680
And in addition to these that
come sort of out of the box,

00:34:19.680 --> 00:34:22.590
we also have custom evaluators,
which is an excellent way

00:34:22.590 --> 00:34:23.970
to then extend this into things

00:34:23.970 --> 00:34:27.750
that are very specific
for your use cases.

00:34:27.750 --> 00:34:29.670
And the great thing
is, as I mentioned,

00:34:29.670 --> 00:34:32.070
is a lot of these evaluators
are available for you

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:35.340
within Azure automatically, or
you can build your custom ones.

00:34:35.340 --> 00:34:38.850
And all of them can be
measured and evaluated

00:34:38.850 --> 00:34:41.340
within the Azure
Foundry interfaces

00:34:41.340 --> 00:34:43.230
as well as programmatically.

00:34:43.230 --> 00:34:45.540
So you have all those
evaluation metrics available

00:34:45.540 --> 00:34:46.890
for your agents to use.

00:34:46.890 --> 00:34:50.010
This allows you to automate
and be able to review things

00:34:50.010 --> 00:34:53.400
in real time, as well as being
able to look over a period

00:34:53.400 --> 00:34:55.350
of time, which will really
help as you're looking

00:34:55.350 --> 00:34:56.880
at drift and as things change.

00:34:56.880 --> 00:34:59.970
Or if you need to make a
change, switch to the latest,

00:34:59.970 --> 00:35:02.460
a new model that's come out,
a new version of a model,

00:35:02.460 --> 00:35:04.440
or make a change
within your code.

00:35:04.440 --> 00:35:08.700
These evaluations are what let
you see how well it will perform

00:35:08.700 --> 00:35:12.810
your full agentic system, even
if you're making changes.

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:15.150
And so, again, this is why
we think evaluations are

00:35:15.150 --> 00:35:17.100
so important, and we really need

00:35:17.100 --> 00:35:19.110
to see those early
on in the process.

00:35:19.110 --> 00:35:20.250
Don't wait till the end.

00:35:20.250 --> 00:35:22.800
This is something that
should be built all the way

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:27.090
through as you're building
out your agentic solutions.

